    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>A cold for four days, and his home blood pressure readings went up. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Sonny Liston came in without an appointment because of a runny and blocked nose, sore throat, mild cough and aches for four days, and home blood pressure readings that rose to 150 to 160 over 95 to 100 mmHg in the past three days after running between 125 and 130 systolic. Three days ago he bought a cold tablet at the store by the picture on the box and has taken two tablets three or four times a day; nobody read him the label. The box he brought contains pseudoephedrine 30 mg. He has felt jittery and slept poorly since. No fever above 38 °C, chest pain, breathlessness, ear pain or headache. He has taken his blood pressure tablet and naltrexone every morning and has not had alcohol this week. Two sparring partners at the gym have colds. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Four days ago; blood pressure up for three days, since starting the cold tablet</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Runny, blocked nose, sore throat, mild dry cough, aches</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Pseudoephedrine 30 mg, two tablets three or four times a day, bought by the picture on the box</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Jittery, poor sleep; no fever, chest pain, breathlessness or headache</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Colds at the gym; on amlodipine, valsartan and naltrexone; no alcohol this week</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

