<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Common cold, with blood pressure raised by a decongestant (J06.9)</content>. A viral upper respiratory infection; no sign of bacterial sinusitis, strep throat or pneumonia, so no antibiotic. The rise in blood pressure and the jitteriness follow the pseudoephedrine; there are no warning symptoms. [S]<list><item>Stop the cold tablet today.</item><item>Saline nasal spray, warm drinks, honey for the cough.</item><item>Acetaminophen 500 mg, one or two tablets every six hours as needed, no more than four tablets a day because he drinks.</item><item>Avoid decongestants (pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine) and pain pills such as ibuprofen and naproxen, which raise blood pressure; avoid cough syrups with codeine, which naltrexone blocks.</item><item>A picture card of medicines to avoid, and ask the pharmacist to read any label to him.</item><item>Home readings each morning; call if above 160/100 mmHg after two days off the tablet, or at once for chest pain, severe headache or breathlessness.</item><item>No sparring until the fever and aches are gone.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertension, stage 2 (I10)</content>. Raised by the decongestant on top of good control. [S]<list><item>Continue amlodipine 10 mg and valsartan 160 mg; no change.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); AHRQ Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit, 3rd Edition (2024); Outpatient Clinical Care for Adults: antibiotic use for common respiratory infections (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse phone call in three days to hear his home readings. Three-month review in two weeks as booked. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-22: three-month review: blood pressure, liver tests and drinking on naltrexone. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
